 "format": "bmc-sensor-audit/redfish-documents/1",
 "_provenance": "CAPTURED. Verbatim Redfish responses from OpenBMC release 2.9.0, release asset obmc-phosphor-image-witherspoon.ubi.mtd (published 2021), booted under QEMU 10.2.1 as machine witherspoon-bmc and captured 2026-08-18. These are the bytes that firmware served, not this project's parsed form, so a test replaying them exercises the walker itself rather than its own output. WHY THIS IMAGE: it is the counterpart to walk_qemu_bletchley.json. That one is current firmware serving only the modern Sensors tree; this one is 2021 firmware serving a populated deprecated Thermal tree while its modern Sensors collection is EMPTY. Current OpenBMC images cannot supply this: bmcweb still ships the handlers but gates them behind the meson feature redfish-allow-deprecated-power-thermal, whose default is disabled and which no machine in the tree enables. SETTLED, DELIBERATELY: the first capture attempt caught the machine with Fans as an empty array because the fan control had not populated it yet, and an empty array reads exactly like a chassis with no fans. The saved capture is asserted to carry 2 Temperatures and 4 Fans before it is written. WHAT THIS IS NOT: evidence about the deprecated POWER path. The Power document here has empty Voltages and PowerSupplies, and its single PowerControl entry is a power-limit object carrying no reading at all, so no real reading has ever exercised that half. It also proves nothing about physical hardware, sensor-population density, or any Redfish dialect other than bmcweb.",
